  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 133 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nA drying trend is expected Sunday as upper ridging rebuilds over the \narea and the trough moves further to the east. No real airmass \nchange is expected with highs continuing to be well above normal \nacross the region into early next week. Another potentially stronger \ntrough is forecast to take shape over the southwest US and head our \ndirection by next Tuesday/Wednesday which will bring our next chance \nof rain to the area. How much rain we see and the exact timing of \nthe trough remain uncertain at this time, with the potential \ninjection of tropical moisture from the eastern Pacific likely \nplaying a key role in rainfall amounts. If everything comes \ntogether this system has the potential to be our best shot at \nwidespread appreciable rainfall our area has seen in many weeks. \nThat said, current NBM percentiles for OKC rain totals show wide \nvariability, ranging from only 0.05\" at the 25th percentile to \nover 2 inches at the 75th percentile. Needless to say, uncertainty\nis high. At least a brief cooldown is also in the cards in the \nwake of this system, with highs forecast to return to near average\nfor this time of year by next Wednesday. \n\nWare",
